{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Problem\n\nA production `rebar create` call failed while committing a ticket event. The command reported the following output.\n\n```text\nError: git commit failed while holding lock: fatal: unable to write new index file\n```\n\nThe failure occurred in `/Users/joeoakhart/docs-epic-finish-20260823` while creating the F30B child of cepivorous-dynamitic-deer. Several ticket writes had succeeded in the same session. An exact-title search after the failure found no created ticket. The tracker then reported a clean status, no `.git/index.lock` existed, the filesystem had 458 GiB available with ample inodes, and an identical create call succeeded as peppery-dire-indianabat. The failed write therefore surfaced to the caller and required an operator retry.\n\n## Prior records\n\nlowcost-unsainted-wallaby records the same Git message when a test timer deletes the lock that Git owns during commit. Its accepted resolution states that production behavior was correct and intentionally excludes this message from retry classification. This occurrence came from a production CLI write and therefore is not resolved by that test-only correction. unheedful-custodial-bluebottle and interior-raspberry-titi cover different write-side filesystem messages. snide-cut-mussel covers `index.lock` contention and stale-lock reclamation.\n\n## Investigation Plan\n\n- Determine which process or recovery path can remove or invalidate the Git-owned index lock during a production ticket commit.\n- Reproduce the failure through the shared ticket-write seam with deterministic coordination instead of a timing sleep.\n- Decide whether the safe correction prevents the invalidation or retries the complete idempotent event commit after this exact failure.\n- Prove that recovery cannot duplicate an event, omit an event, or overwrite a concurrent ticket write.\n- Preserve bounded retry and actionable terminal diagnostics for persistent faults.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] The production root cause is demonstrated with process and lock ownership evidence.\n- [x] A deterministic regression test reproduces the exact failure through a ticket-write entry point.\n- [x] One ticket-write invocation self-recovers from the transient occurrence or prevents the invalidation.\n- [x] Recovery commits exactly one intended event and preserves concurrent events.\n- [x] A persistent occurrence remains bounded and returns an actionable error.\n- [x] Focused store concurrency tests, lint, type checking, and the default test suite pass."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787557367388766001,"uuid":"7a89371b-eee6-4909-b02b-f8c79f6c64fb"}